1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
Moving on to spinal cord lesions, the first disorder is syringomyelia.

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:07,640
This is cystic degeneration of the spinal cord that can arise with trauma,

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,920
or it can also be in association with an Arnold-Chiari malformation.

4
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:18,120
It classically occurs at C8 to T1, and it results in loss of pain and temperature sensation

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,520
with sparing a fine touch and position sense in the upper extremities,

6
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:26,140
and of course in the upper extremities because we're at C8 to T1.

7
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,740
Now, in order to understand this, you do need to know a little bit of normal neural anatomy,

8
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,680
and so let's go back and remind ourselves that this is a simple spinal cord here.

9
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:39,200
Your pain and temperature sensation will actually come in from the outside.

10
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:45,340
It has a cell body here in the sensory ganglion, and then it synapses here in the posterior horn.

11
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:49,260
Now, after synapsing in the posterior horn, it sends off another nerve,

12
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:52,920
which will come down this way, cross over in the anterior white commissure,

13
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,140
and then come around and enter into the spinothalamic tract,

14
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:59,980
and then go up the spinothalamic tract to synapse on the thalamus.

15
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,680
And of course, from the thalamus, it would then synapse on the cortex.

16
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:08,200
So there's actually three independent nerves that allow pain and temperature sensation

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,040
to get to the cortex of the brain.

18
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,460
Now, of course, this would also be present.

19
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,640
So you'd have pain and temperature sensation on the opposite side of the body,

20
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,420
and this would also be present in the exact same way with a cell body here,

21
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:23,200
and then the nerve synapsing here, and then sending off another nerve,

22
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:26,700
which would then again also cross over in the anterior white commissure,

23
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:30,660
and come around to enter into the spinothalamic tract on the opposite side.

24
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:36,740
So what happens in syringomyelia is that you actually get a degeneration of the spinal cord here.

25
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,300
Of course, that's going to knock out the anterior white commissure,

26
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:41,080
and when you knock out the anterior white commissure,

27
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:44,820
you're going to knock out pain and temperature sensation on both sides.

28
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:49,500
And this knock out of pain and temperature sensation is going to occur at the level of C8 to T1,

29
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,120
and so the patients will have loss of pain and temperature sensation in the upper extremity.

30
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:57,060
At the same time, the dorsal column, which is back here,

31
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:01,460
which carries your fine touch and position sense, will not be altered.

32
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,640
And so this is the classic description of syringomyelia.

33
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:08,480
This syrinx could actually expand, and if it expands,

34
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:10,540
it could, for example, knock out the anterior horn.

35
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:15,000
Now recall that what's running in the anterior horn is going to be your motor neurons.

36
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:20,500
You have an upper motor neuron that goes from the cortex and comes down and synapses in the anterior horn,

37
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:24,660
and from the anterior horn, you have an additional neuron that goes out to the muscle.

38
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:29,980
And so if the syrinx expands and then knocks out the lower motor neuron that's going to the muscle,

39
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,700
you'll get signs of lower motor neuron disorder,

40
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:38,720
such as muscle weakness with atrophy, decreased tone, impaired reflexes, etc.

41
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:41,860
And so these are some of your classic lower motor neuron signs.

42
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,820
Now at the same time, you could take this one step further,

43
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,540
and the syrinx could further expand to knock out something here called the lateral horn.

44
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:54,440
So the importance of knocking out the lateral horn is that what runs through the lateral horn

45
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,020
is something called the hypothalamal spinal tract,

46
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:01,080
but more importantly, the hypothalamal spinal tract carries sympathetic input to the face.

47
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:06,320
Recall that in the hypothalamal spinal tract, basically you have a nerve that arises from the hypothalamus

48
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:09,720
and synapses on the lateral horn here at T1,

49
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,760
and then from the lateral horn, you eventually have a couple more neurons

50
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,960
that eventually then go and provide sympathetic input to the face.

51
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:20,660
Now if this syrinx, which of course is occurring at C8 to T1,

52
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:22,920
were to knock out the lateral horn at T1,

53
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:26,220
you would then get the classic features of Horner syndrome.

54
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:31,320
And recall that Horner syndrome basically includes ptosis, meiosis, and anhidrosis.

55
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:35,880
And again, ptosis being a droopy eyelid, meiosis being a constricted pupil,

56
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:39,680
and anhidrosis being decreased sweating of the face.

57
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,740
And so these are some of the other things that can occur with the syringomyelia,

58
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:45,960
and all of this is high yield because it all ties into neural anatomy.

59
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:50,840
Now I've put a little table in your book with spinal cord tracks for you to review,

60
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:56,200
but basically I've highlighted the high yields of syringomyelia with the image that I've drawn here.

61
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:01,060
So we say that it usually occurs at C8 to T1, therefore it's going to involve the upper extremity.

62
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:06,420
You get loss of pain and temperature sensation with sparing a fine touch and position sense.

63
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,460
If it expands, you'll get involvement of other tracks,

64
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:11,860
and this could knock out the anterior horn,

65
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,280
resulting in a lower motor neuron problem with muscle atrophy with weakness,

66
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:19,100
decreased tone and impaired reflexes.

67
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:23,360
And at the same time, if it further expands, you can also knock out the lateral horn.

68
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:27,320
And when you knock out the lateral horn, you're going to knock out the sympathetic input to the face,

69
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:30,300
giving you ptosis, meiosis, and anhidrosis.

70
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:36,940
Moving on to the next disorder, poliomyelitis is damaged to the anterior motor horn due to a poliovirus infection.

71
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:40,680
Let's go back and remind ourselves that polio has a fecal-oral transmission.

72
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,680
Polio then infects the oropharynx and the small bowel.

73
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:47,220
That gives you fevers, sore throat, abdominal pain with nausea and vomiting.

74
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:50,300
And then it can eventually spread to the CNS via the blood.

75
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:54,840
Now, when it spreads to the CNS via the blood, it eventually gets into the anterior motor horn,

76
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:56,740
and that's where it causes its damage.

77
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:00,080
So, again, if you draw a simple spinal cord,

78
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:04,740
then the problem is you've knocked out the anterior motor horn here.

79
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,960
And, of course, from the anterior motor horn was coming the neuron that was going to go out to the skeletal muscle.

80
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:13,240
And so you're going to knock out the lower motor neuron.

81
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:17,860
Now, when you knock out the lower motor neuron, you get flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy.

82
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:20,300
You get weakness with decreased tone.

83
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,160
You get impaired reflexes.

84
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,860
And you get a negative Babinski sign, i.e., downward-going toes.

85
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:30,600
The same exact thing can occur in a disorder called Wurndig-Hoffman disease.

86
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:36,160
However, instead of it being due to polio, it's due to an inherited degeneration of the anterior motor horn.

87
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,400
This is autosomal recessive, and it presents as a floppy baby.

88
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:45,160
And here I've described this in your text by telling you that you get an inherited degeneration of the anterior motor horn.

89
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:50,420
Again, this is autosomal recessive, floppy baby, and death occurs within a few years after birth.

90
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:56,420
Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is a degenerative disorder of upper and lower motor neurons.

91
00:05:56,940 --> 00:06:02,140
You get degeneration of the anterior motor horn, which then leads to lower motor neuron signs.

92
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:12,460
So the lower motor neuron signs include flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy, you get fasciculations, weakness with decreased tone, impaired reflexes, and a negative Babinski.

93
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:20,320
So it's as if everything kind of decreases, where you have this flaccid paralysis with decreased tone, with impaired reflexes, and downward-going toes.

94
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:25,300
And because you involve the lateral corticospinal tract, that leads to upper motor neuron signs.

95
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:31,460
And with upper motor neuron signs, you get spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia, increased muscle tone, and upward-going toes.

96
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:32,780
So it's as if things go up.

97
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:37,580
And this is the key summary for board examination purposes, i.e., upper and lower motor neuron signs.

98
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:47,020
Now, this can somewhat resemble syringomyelia, because recall that syringomyelia involves C8 to T1, and so it would involve the upper extremities and could involve the hands.

99
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:53,400
And in syringomyelia, you could have damage to the anterior horn, and so that could also result in atrophy and weakness of the hands.

100
00:06:53,400 --> 00:07:04,180
However, of course, in syringomyelia, you're going to have loss of pain and temperature sensation, whereas in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, you would have no loss of pain or temperature sensation.

101
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:09,100
And so this is an important distinction between ALS and syringomyelia.

102
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:13,480
Now, most cases of ALS are sporadic, arising in middle-aged adults.

103
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:23,400
However, one very high-yield point for the purposes of board exams is that in familial cases, there can be a zinc-copper superoxide dismutase mutation.

104
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:30,280
And that's very high-yield, because recall that superoxide dismutase is an important mechanism of removing free radicals.

105
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:40,380
Remember that oxygen has the ability to produce superoxide-free radical, and that free radical would be converted to hydrogen peroxide by superoxide dismutase.

106
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:46,880
Now, if you lose superoxide dismutase, you're going to have increased free radical damage within the cells.

107
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:53,380
And so in this particular case, free radical injury could lead to damage in the neurons, and examiners love to go after that.

108
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:57,800
Friedrich ataxia is a degenerative disorder of the cerebellum and spinal cord tracts.

109
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:01,880
Because the cerebellum's involved, you get ataxia, hence the word ataxia in the name.

110
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:07,060
And also, because the spinal cord tracts are involved, you get loss of vibratory sense and proprioception.

111
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:11,400
You get muscle weakness in the lower extremities, loss of the deep tendon reflexes.

112
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:15,680
So the key thing here is ataxia plus multiple spinal cord tracts.

113
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:22,980
Now, this is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by an unstable trinucleotide repeat in the frataxin gene.

114
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:27,420
High-yield to note, the frataxin gene is essential for mitochondrial iron regulation.

115
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:33,680
And, of course, if you cannot regulate iron properly, you increase the risk of developing free radicals via the fentanyl reaction.

116
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:38,140
And so, again, here you would also get free radical damage to the neurons.

117
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:40,100
Now, this presents an early childhood.

118
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:44,560
And another high-yield dissociation is that these patients can have hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.

119
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,800
Examiners like to go after that as well.

120
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:49,360
And that concludes our discussion of spinal cord lesions.

